This will now be my favorite way to explain to people why biosecurity policy is really, really important

@tomieinlove wrote:
> It’s all fun and games until Mythos 2 finds vulnerabilities in the human genome.

i see this kind of just universe reasoning about startups and whatnot but i think it’s wishful thinking. there may be one company that ends up dominating most of the world economy and hopefully is run as some sort of regulated utility

@Suhail wrote:
> Possibly the thing we will most realize looking back: intelligence was so big that lots of companies were going to succeed. It's not so simply bucketed into chatgpt and claude code. x.com/OfficialLoganK…

The funny thing is people rate their own economic circumstances far more favorably now than they did during the Great Recession. This lines up with the data. It's the external economy that rates poorly. Explanations that rely on people not doing well individually don't fit.

@StatisticUrban wrote:
> At the meta level, the vibecession question is simple: either you believe the economy is worse than during the Great Recession, as consumer sentiment implies, despite ~0 empirical evidence to this effect, or you agree that there's a gap between sentiment and material conditions.
